  "composition": [
    {
      "id": "new:b1",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "markup": "Your reasoning gives a strong case for the <e ref=\"src:f2NhuYKwh8gSfjOXGtg-xw:e5\">maintained ship</e>, but <b>“must” is stronger than the premises establish</b>.",
      "ranges": [],
      "textStyle": null,
      "margins": null
    },
    {
      "id": "new:b2",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "markup": "<annotation id=\"new:s1\" type=\"dialectic/qualification\">The missing premise is that uninterrupted use and <e ref=\"src:f2NhuYKwh8gSfjOXGtg-xw:e3\">historical continuity</e> are sufficient for <e ref=\"src:f2NhuYKwh8gSfjOXGtg-xw:e9\">numerical identity</e>—being the very same individual. With that premise, your conclusion follows. But the premise itself needs defending: continued history is evidence for your chosen identity criterion, not proof that this is the only correct criterion.</annotation>",
      "ranges": [],
      "textStyle": null,
      "margins": null
    },
    {
      "id": "new:b3",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "markup": "Likewise, <b>“<e ref=\"src:f2NhuYKwh8gSfjOXGtg-xw:e6\">material continuity</e> is irrelevant” does not follow</b>. Ordinary repairs suggest that retaining every original plank is not necessary for a ship to persist. That does not show that original material has no significance whatsoever. The <e ref=\"src:f2NhuYKwh8gSfjOXGtg-xw:e8\">reconstructed ship</e> makes that competing consideration vivid, without automatically winning the identity claim.",
      "ranges": [],
      "textStyle": null,
      "margins": null
    },
    {
      "id": "new:b4",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "markup": "A more defensible formulation is: <i>“I favour the maintained ship because its continuous history seems more important to vessel identity than retaining the original material.”</i> That states a reasoned position without treating the disputed criterion as already settled.",
      "ranges": [],
      "textStyle": null,
      "margins": null
    }
  ],
  "anchors": [
    {
